e we see  significantly better sensitivity with  simple western compared to western blot  and um and uh  that's reflected in some of the papers  that have been published i can think of  one because it's someone i worked with  directly  as an fas a group out of cal state 410  that published a paper on on muscle  fibers where they were able to get  single fiber resolution of their targets  which had been struggled to achieve with  western blot  but because of uh working with the west  system um they were able to do that and  then doing so they were able to really  tease apart  um specific muscle fiber related um  signaling that  otherwise has been convoluted by the  fact that you always had to aggregate  um fibers able to do those studies so  that sensitivity can make a pretty big  difference  um in your in your work for sure  great thank you so much so dr aw we're  going to move um  excuse me we're going to move back over  to you how does the activity  of dcbp1 compare to other degraders  of uh remote bromodomain  proteins like dbets  yeah so um you know  we've spent we spent a lot of time  studying brd4 and the effects of bre4  inhibitors and  degraders on gene expression and  chromatin structures  um we haven't done much work comparing  you know the dc vp1 uh compound with the  dbet compounds  um we can imagine that you know the  activity of  of brd4 enhancers can be you know quite  similar to the activity of  cvp and p300 but also likely very  distinct um  brd4 has very specific relationships to  recruitment of ptfe  and activating elongation of the rna  polymerase complex  um and so we can imagine that you know  complete degradation of brd4 may have  very specific or  distinct effects than a degrader of cvt  and p300 and may lead to opportunities  potentially for  synergistic activity on enhancers and  and promoters together  but we have not uh as of yet done  comprehensive head-to-head analysis on  of the two compound series  great thank you so we do have time for a  couple more questions so  dr geiger we'll move back over to you um  what data do you typically see people  report out  um electro for it uh excuse me  electro for ferrograms or that western  blot style view  uh yeah i guess it kind of depends on  personal preference  um i think what i've seen mostly is  uh when people are internally analyzing  their data  they quickly become acquainted with that  electro-ferrogram view  uh it's it's truly sort of the  quantitative way to look at your data  and so as you get more familiar with it  you'll you'll find  that you really see a value in that way  of reporting out the data  in terms of just the power to be able to  uh quantitate it  visualize it understand any kind of like  variability and stuff like that  um the disadvantage of it of course is  just that there's uh  it's difficult to put a lot of data in a  single small space uh the  classic western block quote unquote view  um gives you that power  and that's the reason like when i was  reporting my data out and talking about  quantitation i was showing you guys  electric fairgrounds but then when  dr ott was showing his data where he has  a large number of samples that he wants  to show in a single  single slide uh it's advantageous to  throw up that uh blot for you  um so a lot of times with papers we do  see that people use the blot view  just to be able to encapsulate the  information in the small space  but when you're looking at your own data  you'll you'll find very quickly  that you're going to really prefer and  appreciate that electroparagram view  great thank you so much um back over to  you dr ott  how's your work with dcpv1 or analog  shown any selective degradation for cbp  or p300 over the other  yeah that's a great question um so yeah  obviously it would be  fantastic to be able to develop even  more selective tools for one versus the  other  um and the answer is yes we do see um  certain contexts and and some analogs  that actually show  selectivity of one versus the other  we're trying to understand the um  molecular recognition events that that  mediate that seal activity  um and so we're actually working on that  now  wonderful thank you and how about did  they alter the heat  denaturing step for the west samples  i believe that's probably a question for  us um uh  no we did not we did not alter any  um denaturing steps  great thank you so much okay um  looks like we've got time for maybe one  or two more questions so  dr uh what is the optimal dose and time  point  i should use dcvp1 in my cell system  in order to study direct effects of p300  cbp loss yeah another good question so i  think  um typically in most  cell systems we see almost complete  at least by os analysis complete  degradation  uh of both cbp and p300 within  uh you know two to two to three hours of  treatment  at about 100 nanomolar so i think that's  a good um  dose and time point to start um but with  any cell system you'll want to  tune it based on yourself your your cell  type  we have noticed some cell types um where  uptake of the molecule is slightly  attenuated so  the dynamics take a little bit longer in  order to  uh to cause degradation um but you'll  want to be careful  in terms of doing analysis at longer  time points because  you know we've noticed by proteomics  analysis that in some cell lines  you know at six hours you see massive  effects on the proteome beyond just cbt  and p300 so if you're looking to measure  you know sort of acute primary effects  of the loss of cvp and p300 you'll want  to keep  the time points short and sort of tune  it based on your cell system  wonderful thank you so much um so here's  another question so for the immunoblot  of cbp  and p300 did you do multiplexing with  insulin and gap dh  and which one do you recommend for  multiplexing  with cbp p300 and simple western  yeah so we typically multiplex with  zinculin because we use the high  molecular weight kit  um and vinculin as a as a as a 